235:Closest to LSA are methods that organise the keys by semantic content rather than by position. A few early works train at short context, with Reformer ([Kitaev et al., 2020](#bib.bib36)) hashing keys into buckets, Routing Transformer ([Roy et al., 2021](#bib.bib68)) learning k-means centroids, and Treeformer ([Madaan et al., 2023](#bib.bib52)) routing each query down a learned tree. Most other works here only operate at inference time: ClusterKV ([Liu et al., 2025b](#bib.bib48)) and RetroInfer ([Chen et al., 2026](#bib.bib8)) cluster the keys during prefill and score every centroid at decode (flat search), Squeezed Attention ([Hooper et al., 2025b](#bib.bib25)), Multipole Attention ([Hooper et al., 2025a](#bib.bib24)) and LycheeCluster ([Li et al., 2026](#bib.bib43)) search two levels (fixed search), and IceCache ([Mao et al., 2026b](#bib.bib54)) descends a tree of keys (tree search), while RetrievalAttention ([Liu et al., 2025a](#bib.bib47)) links the keys in a nearest-neighbour graph. All of these inference methods are training-free, only Squeezed Attention speeds up prefill, and only partially, and none are evaluated at ultra-long context (above 1M tokens).
329:Parallelism. We integrate LSA into SGLang with support for tensor, expert, and pipeline parallelism, and for context parallelism in both prefill and decode. Context parallelism splits the KV cache across ranks by token, but LSA replicates its bucket cache on every rank. Every rank thus builds the same index and selects the same index list. The ranks only exchange the index keys of the newest tokens. This design choice trades memory for communication. At 10M tokens on GLM-5.3-Flash with context parallelism over 8 GPUs, each rank holds a 1.25 GiB share of the 10 GiB KV cache of a layer and the full 246 MiB bucket cache.
349:In this section, we describe the models, the methods we compare, and their settings. Then, we evaluate the accuracy of D-LSA up to 10M tokens, and its performance per attention layer and end to end. Finally, we show that N-LSA can be trained natively at an accuracy close to full attention.
380:Design parameters. Table [2](#S4.T2 "Table 2 ‣ 4.1 Setup ‣ 4 Evaluation") lists the parameters of every method per model. For D-LSA, we found that a budget of k\=4096k=4096 long-range keys holds accuracy past 1M tokens better than smaller budgets, and these extra keys cost little next to the scan of the indexer they replace. On GLM-5.3-Flash, D-LSA (Flat) selects k\=2048k=2048 long-range keys for queries before position 1M and k\=4096k=4096 beyond. We found that the smaller budget keeps accuracy below 1M tokens while making the attention faster. Tuning the budget further, for example per layer or per request, is left to future work. A small index space of d(idx)\=64d^{(\\mathrm{idx})}=64 dimensions keeps the scoring and the clustering cheap, and we found wider spaces to add little accuracy.
382:Table 2: Parameters.  Block Sparse Attention (Flat),  Block Sparse Attention (Tree),  D-LSA (Flat) and  D-LSA (Tree) per model (symbols in Table [5](#A2.T5 "Table 5 ‣ Appendix B Method Details")). The search is flat () or tree (), over blocks of npooln\_{\\mathrm{pool}} consecutive keys or over buckets. Block Sparse Attention (Tree) always keeps the first, previous and current blocks among its pp blocks. On GLM-5.3-Flash, D-LSA (Flat) uses the first kk and pp below 1M tokens and the second beyond.
596:Indexer distillation. We train on 352M tokens of documents of 16k to 1M tokens from seven domains, for 48k steps, each on 128 queries sampled from one document, with a learning rate of 3⋅10−43\\cdot 10^{-4}. This is a fraction of the 2.1B tokens of the dense warm-up of DSA. In Equation [2](#S3.E2 "In 3.3 D-LSA ‣ 3 Latent Sparse Attention (LSA)"), the target AiA\_{i} keeps only the 2,048 keys with the most attention weight per query, renormalised over them, while A^i\\hat{A}\_{i} still spans all of ℛi\\mathcal{R}\_{i}, and τ\\tau is calibrated per layer before the first step. Unlike DSA, we do not follow the distillation by training the whole model under sparse attention (sparse training), a step D-LSA also supports but we omit here due to its cost. We provide further details about the composition and construction of the dataset in Appendix [C](#A3 "Appendix C Training Data").
598:Benchmarks. We evaluate accuracy in three categories, each with a representative set of benchmarks. For knowledge, we use MMLU-Pro ([Wang et al., 2024](#bib.bib77)) (a stratified sample of 2,000 questions) and GPQA-Diamond ([Rein et al., 2024](#bib.bib67)). For reasoning, we use AIME25 (four repeats) and MATH500 ([Lightman et al., 2024](#bib.bib46)) (100 problems). For long context, we use LongBench v2 ([Bai et al., 2025](#bib.bib3)) (60 questions) and the 13 tasks of RULER ([Hsieh et al., 2024](#bib.bib26)) from 4k to 10M tokens (100 prompts per task up to 1M and 20 beyond). Evaluating each benchmark in full at ultra-long context is prohibitively expensive, and we therefore use the subsets given in parentheses.
602:Model sampling. We configure each model’s sampling as recommended by its model card with generation up to 163,840 tokens (Appendix [E](#A5 "Appendix E Accuracy")). Qwen-3.8-27B reaches 10M tokens with YaRN ([Peng et al., 2024](#bib.bib63)) at factor 40 (42 for PaperTrail at 10M), while GLM-5.3-Flash needs no extension as it has no explicit positional encoding.
608:Figure 5: D-LSA accuracy is on par with full attention and sparse attention baselines on downstream tasks. Shown on knowledge, reasoning and long-context tasks up to 10M tokens. Baseline scores are given with their 95% confidence interval.
614:Long context. On Qwen-3.8-27B, D-LSA (Flat) stays within 5 points of baseline Full Attention up to 1M tokens and exceeds it at 10M tokens, at 63.9 against 59.9 on RULER and 29.1 against 19.8 on PaperTrail. It also stays above Block Sparse Attention (Flat) on RULER from 128k tokens on, in a fair comparison, as both indexers are distilled the same way.
616:On GLM-5.3-Flash, D-LSA (Flat) stays within 3 points of baseline sparse attention up to 1M tokens, trails it by up to 8 points at 2M to 5M tokens, and is within 4 points on RULER at 10M tokens. This comparison favours the baseline, which was trained together with the model, while D-LSA (Flat) is distilled from attention that is already sparse (with no finetuning of the base model itself). On both models, MultiKey 2, MultiKey 3 and CWE fall the most for every method, the baselines included. We break down RULER by task and PaperTrail by tier in Appendix [E](#A5 "Appendix E Accuracy").
618:Design comparison. D-LSA (Tree) follows D-LSA (Flat) on Qwen-3.8-27B, but loses accuracy past 1M tokens on GLM-5.3-Flash, at 55.8 against 66.8 on RULER at 10M tokens. Block Sparse Attention (Tree) falls behind from 512k tokens, to about 63 on RULER at 1M tokens on both models, against 78 and 88 for D-LSA (Tree), and we therefore do not evaluate it beyond 1M tokens. Large blocks fall earlier (Appendix [E.6](#A5.SS6 "E.6 Block Size ‣ Appendix E Accuracy")). D-LSA (Training-Free), without any training, stays close to D-LSA (Flat) on Qwen-3.8-27B, but trails it on GLM-5.3-Flash.
620:D-LSA follows the accuracy of the baselines, showing that our semantic clustering can preserve accuracy up to 10M tokens. However, we do observe a steep drop in accuracy beyond 512k tokens for Block Sparse Attention (Tree).
624:Figure 6: D-LSA speedup of one global attention layer. Prefill and decode speedup over the baseline attention (log scale), Full Attention for Qwen-3.8-27B and Block Sparse Attention (Flat) for GLM-5.3-Flash, required memory at 10M with one index key per block of four keys for Block Sparse Attention (Flat), and prefill scaling up to 100M (log-log), extrapolated beyond 10M (shaded).
630:Figure [6](#S4.F6 "Figure 6 ‣ 4.3 Performance ‣ 4 Evaluation") shows that the prefill speedup of D-LSA grows with the context. On GLM-5.3-Flash, it rises from 3.5×\\times at 1M to 22.7×\\times at 10M tokens over baseline sparse attention, and on Qwen-3.8-27B to 72.4×\\times at 10M tokens over baseline Full Attention. Below 1M tokens, the gain is smaller, at 2.1×\\times at 512k tokens, as the scan of every block is still cheap. Extrapolated beyond 10M tokens, the prefill of D-LSA (Tree) grows subquadratically and overtakes the flat search, and at 100M tokens it is predicted to be more than 90×\\times faster than baseline sparse attention.
632:Decode follows the same trend, with a speedup up to 4.6×\\times over baseline sparse attention and 42.5×\\times over baseline Full Attention at 10M tokens, with D-LSA (Tree) decoding with the flat search. On B300, D-LSA reaches 12.5×\\times and 42.9×\\times in prefill and 5.2×\\times and 18.4×\\times in decode at 10M tokens.
634:D-LSA also requires less memory for its indexer. Baseline sparse attention caches an index key for every block of four keys, while D-LSA caches one centroid in its smaller index space for every bucket of about eight keys, together with the id of each key. At 10M tokens, the index of D-LSA (Flat) takes 246 MiB per layer against 331 MiB in GLM-5.3-Flash and 642 MiB in QSA. Larger buckets would shrink the index of D-LSA further.
636:Table 3: D-LSA speedup in production setting. Time to first token (TTFT) and time per output token (TPOT) of one request under SGLang.  Full Attention,  Block Sparse Attention (Flat),  D-LSA (Flat) and  D-LSA (Tree). Each block’s first row gives the baseline’s times and the others their speedup over it, with D-LSA at 10M in bold. Qwen-3.8-27B is served with tensor parallelism (TP8) and decode context parallelism (DCP8) on H200 and with TP8 on B300, GLM-5.3-Flash with either context parallelism (CP8, with DCP8 and expert parallelism EP8) or TP8. Without DCP, Block Sparse Attention’s prefill indexer runs whole on every TP rank. All models run on a single node, except TP8 for GLM-5.3-Flash at 10M on H200 (2 nodes with PP2); a B300 node holds it on one.
