962:End-to-end. Table [3](#S4.T3 "Table 3 ‣ 4.3 Performance ‣ 4 Evaluation") shows that the speedup of one layer carries over to the whole model at ultra-long context. On GLM-5.3-Flash, D-LSA (Flat) is on par with baseline sparse attention up to 1M tokens with CP8 and already 1.5×\\times faster to first token at 1M with TP8, and at 10M tokens reduces the TTFT by 2.2×\\times and the TPOT by 2.1×\\times with CP8, and by 5.8×\\times and 1.9×\\times with TP8. On Qwen-3.8-27B, it reduces them by 9.2×\\times and 2.9×\\times at 10M tokens against baseline Full Attention. On B300, D-LSA (Flat) reduces the TTFT and the TPOT at 10M tokens by 1.6×\\times and 2.2×\\times with CP8 and by 4.8×\\times and 2.6×\\times with TP8 on GLM-5.3-Flash, and by 5.7×\\times and 1.5×\\times on Qwen-3.8-27B. The speedup of the whole model is expected to be smaller than that of one layer, as only the global attention layers become faster, while the other layers keep their cost.
1000:128k
1124:Setup. We train a hybrid model of about 0.5B parameters with 24 layers, in which every fourth layer is an attention layer and the others are Gated DeltaNet layers ([Yang et al., 2025b](#bib.bib83)). Each attention layer has 8 query heads over a single key-value head of 128 dimensions, without positional encoding. We pre-train with a standard Warmup-Stable-Decay (WSD) learning rate schedule ([Hu et al., 2024](#bib.bib27)), on 100B tokens of FineWeb-Edu ([Penedo et al., 2024](#bib.bib62)) at a context of 4k tokens in the Warmup and Stable phases, followed by 12B tokens of LongDocs in the Decay phase, at 128k tokens (Appendix [C](#A3 "Appendix C Training Data")). We compare N-LSA with Full Attention, Sliding Window Attention (SWA), and the native forms of block sparse attention, NSA ([Yuan et al., 2025](#bib.bib85)) for Block Sparse Attention (Flat) and PISA ([Tang et al., 2026](#bib.bib71)) for Block Sparse Attention (Tree), all in the same model architecture and on the same data. Every sparse method attends to about 2,560 keys per query. N-LSA selects k\=2048k=2048 long-range keys with flat search, next to a window of 512 keys and 4 sink keys. SWA attends to a window of 2,560 keys and 4 sink keys, NSA attends to 32 selected blocks of 64 keys and a window of 512 keys, and PISA attends to 40 blocks of 64 keys, which always include the first block and the two most recent blocks (window). We train N-LSA with flat search only, and leave native training with tree search to future work.
1126:Figure [7](#S4.F7 "Figure 7 ‣ 4.4 Native Training ‣ 4 Evaluation") shows that N-LSA reaches the validation loss of Full Attention throughout the decay phase at 128k token context, at 1.085 against 1.085, while PISA, NSA and SWA trail at 1.100, 1.117 and 1.154. During pre-training at 4k tokens, all methods stay within 0.004 of each other, as their keys cover most of the context. Table [4](#S4.T4 "Table 4 ‣ 4.4 Native Training ‣ 4 Evaluation") confirms this on downstream tasks. On commonsense reasoning ([Clark et al., 2018](#bib.bib10); [Zellers et al., 2019](#bib.bib90)), all models are on par. On recall ([Arora et al., 2024](#bib.bib2)), N-LSA stays close to Full Attention and ahead of the other sparse methods. On RULER, N-LSA stays within 3.5 points of Full Attention up to 128k tokens, at 32.2 against 34.7 at 128k tokens, while NSA and PISA fall to 19.2 and 14.6, and SWA to 3.0 once the context outgrows its window.
1128:N-LSA also trains faster than Full Attention at long context. For one attention layer on a single GPU, the forward and backward pass of N-LSA is 1.1×\\times faster than Full Attention with FlashAttention-3 ([Shah et al., 2024](#bib.bib69)) at 64k tokens and 1.6×\\times faster at 128k tokens (Figure [7](#S4.F7 "Figure 7 ‣ 4.4 Native Training ‣ 4 Evaluation"), left). End to end, the whole model completes its decay phase 1.24×\\times faster at the same validation loss (Figure [7](#S4.F7 "Figure 7 ‣ 4.4 Native Training ‣ 4 Evaluation"), right), as only every fourth layer is an attention layer. At shorter contexts, the selection of the keys costs more than it saves. Its peak memory is higher, at 6.8 GiB against 5.8 GiB per layer at 128k tokens, partly because N-LSA keeps the indices of the selected keys of every query for the backward pass, which grow linearly with the context.
1136:We observed tree search to lose accuracy faster than flat search past 1M tokens on GLM-5.3-Flash, while it overtakes flat search in decode only from about 5M tokens with one request, and is predicted to do so in prefill only beyond 10M tokens. It remains open whether sparse training would close this gap beyond indexer distillation.
1138:We train N-LSA at a small scale, with about 0.5B parameters, up to 128k tokens and with flat search only. Scaling native training to larger models, longer contexts and tree search remains open.
1142:At ultra-long context, the cost of sparse attention lies in finding which keys to attend to. We introduce LSA, which clusters the keys in a learned latent space and replaces the scan of every key with a search over the clusters, flat or along a tree. D-LSA distils LSA into pre-trained models and stays close to the accuracy of their baseline attention up to 10M tokens. At 10M tokens, one attention layer of D-LSA prefills 22.7×\\times and decodes 4.6×\\times faster than sparse attention in GLM-5.3-Flash, and 72.4×\\times and 42.5×\\times faster than Full Attention in Qwen-3.8-27B. End to end, this reduces the time to first token by 2.2×\\times and 9.2×\\times, respectively. N-LSA trains LSA natively on the next-token loss alone, at the accuracy of Full Attention and 1.6×\\times faster per layer at 128k tokens. With tree search, the cost of finding the keys grows subquadratically, which makes LSA a step towards attention that scales well beyond 10M tokens. LSA thus provides a practical way forward for ultra-long context attention, both for distilling pre-trained models and for native training.
1246:Figure 8: Top-kk Oracle Attention on RULER. Qwen-3.8-27B at 128k tokens. Each query attends to a 4-token sink, a 512-token window and the top-kk long-range keys, one list shared by all heads. Process: sum over all heads, pick top-kk, renormalise per head over the shared selection.
1250:To test how many keys a query needs to keep accuracy, we let each query of Qwen-3.8-27B attend only to the sink, the window and its true top-kk keys (Figure [8](#A0.F8 "Figure 8")). At 128k tokens, Top-kk Oracle Attention keeps 98% of the RULER accuracy of Full Attention down to k\=512k=512, while each query attends to 0.8% of the keys. Single-needle retrieval holds down to k\=16k=16, while MultiKey 2, MultiKey 3 and CWE fall first in accuracy. Therefore, sparse attention loses little if it finds the most relevant keys, which is what LSA is designed to do without scoring every key.
1441:    Sequence: a contiguous part of one document, at a particular sequence length from 16k to 1M tokens.
1450:Distillation training. We distil every indexer on a mix of 352M tokens drawn from LongDocs (Figure [9](#A3.F9 "Figure 9 ‣ Appendix C Training Data")). The mix is uniformly sampled across domain and sequence length, with a bias towards longer context windows at 512k and 1M. In particular, we give each sequence length 32M tokens, and twice and four times as many to 512k and 1M. Sequences of 1M tokens thus hold about a third of the training tokens. We experimentally determine this choice to be beneficial, with +0.036 accuracy on RULER at 1M (averaged over 3 seeds, notable lifts in multikey\_2, multikey\_3, +0.35 and +0.15 respectively), while accuracy from 4k to 512k stays roughly identical. Within each length, every domain receives an equal share of the tokens. A domain with too few documents of that length gives its share to the others (parliament stops at 256k tokens and discussion at 512k).
1452:Native training. The decay training stage of N-LSA reads the whole collection rather than the mix, without the long-context agent sessions. The data is tokenised (18.9B tokens over 134,740 documents), and cut into sequence lengths the training requires.
