1497:Questions. Each sample asks 30 scored questions, 10 per tier. A tier 1 question recalls a fact from one paper, a tier 2 question links two papers, and a tier 3 question links three or more papers, often the whole trail. Each sample also asks 6 questions whose answer is not in the papers, about an absent entity, a false premise or a near miss, which we report apart from the score (Table [8](#A5.T8 "Table 8 ‣ E.5 PaperTrail ‣ Appendix E Accuracy")). The model ends its reply with the papers it used and its answer. A number counts within a relative tolerance of 2% by default, and a set of papers is scored by its F1 against the reference set.
1505:Controls. Four controls ask the same questions over parts of a sample: the question alone, the host papers without their insertions, the fabricated papers with the insertions alone, and the background papers alone. By construction, only the fabricated papers and the insertions hold the answers. We validate the controls with GLM-5.3-Flash and D-LSA (Training-Free) in Table [8](#A5.T8 "Table 8 ‣ E.5 PaperTrail ‣ Appendix E Accuracy").
1513:Table [6](#A5.T6 "Table 6 ‣ E.1 Evaluation Setup ‣ Appendix E Accuracy") lists the setup of every benchmark. We sample Qwen-3.8-27B at temperature 1.0 with top-p 0.95, top-k 20, min-p 0 and no presence penalty, and GLM-5.3-Flash at temperature 1.0 with top-p 0.95, as their model cards recommend. On RULER, both models decode greedily with reasoning disabled. On every benchmark with reasoning, at most 4.6% of the replies reach the output cap of 163,840 tokens for any model and method.
1515:Table 6: Evaluation setup. Questions, repeats, generation and metric of every benchmark, with RULER questions per task.
1615:Table [7](#A5.T7 "Table 7 ‣ E.2 Knowledge and Reasoning ‣ Appendix E Accuracy") compares every method with the baselines on knowledge, reasoning and LongBench v2. Every score lies within the 95% confidence interval of the respective baselines.
1617:Table 7: Accuracy compared to the baseline. Baseline scores are in bold. Other rows show the difference to the baseline in points. Differences smaller than the 95% confidence interval (CI) of the baseline are not significant.
1807:Table [8](#A5.T8 "Table 8 ‣ E.5 PaperTrail ‣ Appendix E Accuracy") further reports the questions whose answer is not in the papers, at 512k tokens. Every D-LSA design recognises these questions within 3.7 points of the baseline of its model, at 96.3 to 100.0 against 100.0 for Qwen-3.8-27B and 96.3 for GLM-5.3-Flash.
1809:Table 8: PaperTrail not-in-corpus questions and controls. Score (%) on the questions whose answer is not in the papers at 512k tokens, for  Full Attention,  Block Sparse Attention (Flat) and  (Tree), and  D-LSA (Flat),  (Tree) and  (Training-Free). The controls ask the scored questions over parts of a sample, with GLM-5.3-Flash and D-LSA (Training-Free).
1987:Hardware. We run on NVIDIA H200 (Hopper) and B300 (Blackwell Ultra) GPUs, eight per node (NVLink domain), with the details presented in Table [9](#A6.T9 "Table 9 ‣ Appendix F Performance").
