2253:Attention kernels. D-LSA attends through existing kernels, which only need to read the index lists in a layout of their own. The expansion writes the index list JiJ\_{i} of every query ii as a row of nsink+k+nw\=4612n\_{\\mathrm{sink}}+k+n\_{w}=4612 int32 entries. Each entry is the slot in the KV cache of one key j∈Jij\\in J\_{i}, and the row is padded with −1\-1 when the query has fewer keys to attend to. The index lists of a chunk 𝒞\\mathcal{C} thus form one matrix of |𝒞|\\lvert\\mathcal{C}\\rvert rows. On GLM-5.3-Flash, JiJ\_{i} takes the place of the index list of the model’s own indexer, and the sparse attention of SGLang attends over it unchanged. On Qwen-3.8-27B, we pass every query ii to a paged attention kernel as a sequence of its own: its row is the page table, each page holds one slot, and the sequence length is |Ji|\\lvert J\_{i}\\rvert. Table [10](#A6.T10 "Table 10 ‣ F.1 Kernels ‣ Appendix F Performance") lists the attention kernels we use, from FlashAttention-3 (FA3) ([Shah et al., 2024](#bib.bib69)) and its successor FA4, FlashMLA ([Li et al., 2025](#bib.bib44)), the TRT-LLM kernels of FlashInfer ([Ye et al., 2025](#bib.bib84)) and Triton. Each is the default of SGLang ([Zheng et al., 2024](#bib.bib94)) for its model and GPU, except where that default cannot run. For instance, on B300, D-LSA on Qwen-3.8-27B attends through the Triton kernel of SGLang, as FlashAttention supports pages of a single slot only on Hopper. With CP8, GLM-5.3-Flash decodes through a split-KV sparse kernel that we add to SGLang (Appendix [F.2](#A6.SS2 "F.2 Parallelism ‣ Appendix F Performance")), and on B300 it prefills through a TileLang kernel ([Wang et al., 2025](#bib.bib76)). With decode context parallelism (DCP8, Appendix [F.2](#A6.SS2 "F.2 Parallelism ‣ Appendix F Performance")) on H200, Full Attention and Block Sparse Attention (Flat) on Qwen-3.8-27B decode through Triton kernels.
2255:Table 10: Attention kernels. Kernel through which each method attends, by model, phase and GPU, at TP8 or on one GPU.  Full Attention,  Block Sparse Attention (Flat),   D-LSA (Flat and Tree). Kernels in italics are non-default SGLang options.
2377:Table 11: Indexer kernels. Time of each step of the indexer of D-LSA on one H200, with its CuTe DSL and its Triton kernels, and the regime of its main CuTe DSL kernel.
2505:Table 12: Tree search in decode. Time of the selection of one layer for one decode step, from the scoring of the centroids to the index lists, in µs, with the flat and the tree search, on one H200, replayed from a CUDA graph. The faster search is in bold.
2571:Measurements. Table [11](#A6.T11 "Table 11 ‣ F.1 Kernels ‣ Appendix F Performance") times each step on one H200 with Nsight Compute, in a single profile with the clocks unlocked, with its CuTe DSL and its Triton kernels. The inputs are synthetic, at the shapes we serve: a prefill chunk of 32,768 queries at 10M tokens, a beam of pℓ\=2048p\_{\\ell}=2048 over node buckets of nb,ℓ\=4n\_{b,\\ell}=4, decode with one request at 10M tokens, the Ward clustering of the 64 cluster blocks of one prefill chunk, and the clustering of one node block of ncluster,ℓ\=4096n\_{\\mathrm{cluster},\\ell}=4096 buckets into node buckets. The times are those of the kernels alone, without the PyTorch launches between them. The regime of a step is where its main CuTe DSL kernel sits: bound by compute or by memory, with its share of the bf16 peak or of the HBM bandwidth of Table [9](#A6.T9 "Table 9 ‣ Appendix F Performance"), bound by neither as a radix select over packed integer keys (selection), or limited by its serial rounds (latency). The bucket scan, the largest step of the flat prefill, reaches 49% of the bf16 peak and uses 1% of the HBM bandwidth, so the tiles of queries reread the centroids mostly from the L2 cache. On real inputs, the index queries of 12 held-out documents of 1M tokens and 3 books of 10M tokens on GLM-5.3-Flash, over all its sparse layers, LiL\_{i} holds 1,679 buckets on average at 1M tokens and 1,737 at 10M, never overflows, and falls back, with fewer than pp buckets above θi\\theta\_{i}, for 5×10−55\\times 10^{-5} of the queries at 1M and 10−310^{-3} at 10M. In decode, 3×10−43\\times 10^{-4} of the steps fall back at 1M, mostly as their lists overflow, and none at 10M. The decode scan reads at 3.58 TB/s, 75% of the HBM bandwidth. At 10M tokens, the CuTe DSL kernels are faster than the Triton ones in every step, from 1.02×\\times for candidate scoring, which is bound by the HBM bandwidth in both languages, to 4.4×\\times for the threshold estimate. On B300, they are faster by up to 4.3×\\times in every step but level 0 of the tree search, where the two languages tie. Of the 22 ms that CuTe DSL saves on a prefill chunk at 10M tokens, 13.4 ms come from the bucket scan, mostly from overlapping its epilogue with its products, and 6.8 ms from the threshold estimate, whose Triton version writes the scores of its sample to HBM and reads them twice. The Triton versions of other steps take more launches or more passes: the node-bucket clustering takes three launches per round and reads two flags on the host every eight rounds (3.4×\\times), and the expansion finds its cut one bit per pass rather than one byte (1.75×\\times). Table [11](#A6.T11 "Table 11 ‣ F.1 Kernels ‣ Appendix F Performance") sums the kernel times of the four launches of the decode select (the sample, the threshold, the scan and the listing), 71 µs at 10M tokens. Replayed from a CUDA graph, where programmatic dependent launch overlaps them, they take 14.7 µs at 1M tokens and 60.7 µs at 10M, against 36.4 µs and 146.6 µs in Triton. With the expansion and the other kernels of the selection, a flat decode step selects in 35 µs at 1M tokens and 86 µs at 10M, and Table [12](#A6.T12 "Table 12 ‣ F.1 Kernels ‣ Appendix F Performance") compares it with the tree search. With one request, the flat search is faster up to about 5M tokens. With 4 and 16 requests, the tree search is faster from about 1M and 256k tokens, and with 16 requests it is 5.6×\\times faster at 10M tokens. As we evaluate one request at a time, every result of D-LSA (Tree) decodes with the flat search, and only its prefill uses the tree. At 1M tokens, the four steps of the flat prefill take 5.2 ms in CuTe DSL against 9.4 ms in Triton. For a whole layer of GLM-5.3-Flash on H200, CuTe DSL makes a prefill chunk at 10M tokens 1.35×\\times faster than Triton.
2581:For both TP and CP, every rank holds the whole bucket cache Bcache(idx)B\_{\\mathrm{cache}}^{(\\mathrm{idx})}, which trades memory for communication (Section [3](#S3 "3 Latent Sparse Attention (LSA)")). Table [13](#A6.T13 "Table 13 ‣ F.2 Parallelism ‣ Appendix F Performance") summarises the communication that the indexer adds for the two mesh configurations. We discuss these in more detail below.
